-8B): DR 15.37→0.93% (↓94%), RV/ET→0%, CF: 0.08→9.90%, R-F1: 8.23→8.36 (NS)\n- DocRED ablation (Qwen3-8B): DR 18.46→10.21% (↓45%), RV→0.03%, grammar-only DR *increases* to 26.17% (latent violation surfacing), R-F1: 7.74→8.23 (p<0.001)\n- Cross-model (Llama-3.1-8B): SciERC DR 13.34→0.08% (↓99.4%), DocRED DR 26.07→2.55% (↓90.2%)\n- 70B model: SciERC DR 6.5→0%, DocRED DR 11.0→1.2% (↓89.1%)\n- WikiEvents EE: EV/RoV→0% across all 3 models, Entity F1 recovers from 0% to 11.7-17.6%\n- Post-hoc filtering: discards 16.8-48.1% for RE, 99.6-100% for EE (non-viable)\n- Fine-tuned composability: RoV→0%, AC 1.8→0.2%, F1 neutral\n- Latency overhead: 13.1% (SciERC), 17.5% (DocRED), ~20% (EE est.)\n- Bootstrap CI: 10,000 iterations for all F1 comparisons\n\n**Analysis sections**:\n- §6.1 Enabling dependency: semantic-only achieves 24% DR reduction vs full system's 94%\n- §6.2 Tightness × RV baseline interaction: explains differential reduction rates across model-dataset combos\n- §6.3 Fallback frequency as constraint coverage metric\n- §6.4 CF tradeoff analysis with error case study\n- §6.5 Error analysis: AC residuals categorized (72% semantic confusion, 12% BPE collision, 16% boundary cases)\n- §6.6 S1 vs S2 strategy comparison\n\n**Violation Taxonomy**: DR (domain/range), RV (relation vocabulary), ET (entity type), CF (cross-field), EV (event vocabulary), RoV (role vocabulary), AC (argument constraint)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe paper has 35 planned experiments (P001-P035). Key ones to check coverage:\n- P001-P004: Violation landscape (SciERC/DocRED × minimal/schema-informed) — covered in §3\n- P005a-P008: SciERC 4-way ablation + S2 — covered in §5.2, §6.6\n- P009-P012: DocRED 4-way ablation — covered in §5.3\n- P013-P016: Llama cross-model — covered in §5.4\n- P017: S2 pilot — superseded, not cited (correct)\n- P018: Post-hoc filtering — covered in §5.7\n- P019: Bootstrap CI — covered in Appendix A\n- P021-P028: WikiEvents EE (3 models × unconstrained/constrained + grammar-only ablation + post-hoc) — covered in §5.6, §5.7\n- P029-P030: 70B cross-scale — covered in §5.5 (P030 DocRED was RUNNING during context creation)\n- P031: Error analysis — covered in §6.4, §6.5\n- P032: EE FT composability — covered in §5.8\n- P033: Grammar-only cross-model EE — covered in Table 8 (but only Mistral in §5.6 text, Qwen/Llama in table)\n- P034: Bootstrap CI for EE — covered in Appendix A Table 11\n- P035: Latency — covered in §5.9, Appendix D\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the two-layer architecture well-motivated? Is the enabling dependency clearly established? Are there methodological gaps?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: \n   - Coverage: Are all necessary experiments present? Any missing baselines or ablations?\n   - Statistical validity: Are significance tests appropriate? Sample sizes adequate?\n   - Is the evaluation fair and comprehensive?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are implementation details sufficient?\n\n4. **Weaknesses**: \n   - CF violations increase (0.08→9.90% on SciERC, up to 21.1% on EE) — is this adequately addressed?\n   - DocRED only 45% DR reduction (Qwen) — is this a significant limitation?\n   - Zero-shot F1 scores are very low (2-11%) — does this affect claim validity?\n   - 70B DocRED results appear to be estimated/running — is this noted?\n   - Any missing experiments or analyses?\n\n5. **Strengths**: What works well?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n### Justification: [2-3 sentences]\n```\n","uuid":"abd2b341-e25e-409a-82b7-6e015017917d","session_id":"0eddf8e3-65e4-4850-9e66-a5c0b106b0fa"}
