{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RDcboYJ3X7aAybygyddnEj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Runtime overhead (inline paragraph, no subsection header to save space)\n2\t\n3\t\\paragraph{Runtime overhead.}\n4\t\\label{sec:runtime}\n5\tFull S1 adds $+$13.1\\% wall-clock overhead on SciERC (Qwen3-8B, zero-shot greedy, RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell); under fine-tuning, overhead drops to $+$2.3\\% (61\\,ms/doc).\n6\tGrammar compile is a one-time 44\\,ms cost.\n7\tDetails in Appendix~\\ref{app:runtime}.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Beam search compatibility.}\n10\t\\label{sec:beam-results}\n11\tBeam search ($k{=}4$) addresses greedy decoding's two residual limitations on SciERC (551 documents): DR drops to 0\\% for calibrated models (Qwen 0.93\\%\\,$\\to$\\,0\\%; Llama 0.08\\%\\,$\\to$\\,0\\%) and CF decreases substantially (Qwen 9.90\\%\\,$\\to$\\,0.53\\%; Llama 6.56\\%\\,$\\to$\\,0.93\\%).\n12\tCombined with LoRA, both calibrated models achieve zero violations across all four metrics on SciERC (Qwen3-8B R-F1\\,=\\,44.23; Llama-3.1-8B R-F1\\,=\\,46.11; Appendix~\\ref{app:beam-search}).\n13\t\n14\t% === 70B cross-scale (PLACEHOLDER — awaiting DocRED 70B results) ===\n15\t%\\paragraph{Cross-scale generalizability.}\n16\t%\\label{sec:70b-results}\n17\t%To evaluate cross-scale generalizability, we test OntoCoDec on Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (AWQ INT4 quantization).\n18\t%On SciERC, the 70B model produces fewer baseline violations than its 8B counterpart (DR 6.5\\% vs.\\ 13.3\\%), yet violations persist; OntoCoDec eliminates them entirely (DR$\\to$0\\%, R-F1 $-$0.03\\,pp).\n19\t%On DocRED, [PLACEHOLDER for 70B DocRED results].\n20\t%Case studies comparing 8B and 70B behavior appear in Appendix~\\ref{app:case-scale}.\n21\t\n22\tAppendix~\\ref{app:case-studies} presents representative case studies illustrating constraint behavior across violation types.\n23\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017RpnbvZEF1ki1hwCUCMPLE","session_id":"0eddf8e3-65e4-4850-9e66-a5c0b106b0fa","uuid":"d3090a64-6a2c-4bbc-be97-64dd871d3046","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:17:44.718Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"afd0ad5d9d6268787","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMVKYmBqUBcXPnRtXNfp5a","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 responds to R2's review","usage":{"total_tokens":46915,"tool_uses":14,"duration_ms":75275},"uuid":"600126fb-e5e1-401b-be84-72bf0b56ce19","session_id":"0eddf8e3-65e4-4850-9e66-a5c0b106b0fa"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMVKYmBqUBcXPnRtXNfp5a","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me formulate my response as R1.\n\n## R1 Response to R2\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\n1. **Incremental novelty relative to type-constrained code generation (W1).** I agree this is the paper's central vulnerability. The \"type system to automaton to logit mask\" pipeline has clear antecedents. The authors do articulate why IE differs (dynamic entity registry, context-dependent constraint targets, non-rectangular type signatures), and the continuation masking for tokenizer-agnostic enforcement is non-trivial engineering. But the conceptual leap is modest. I flagged this in my initial review as well.\n\n2. **\"Universal\" overclaim (W2).** Two IE tasks (RE and EE) with flat type hierarchies do not justify \"universal\" or \"task-agnostic.\" I agree. The WikiEvents extension is valuable but structurally similar (role-conditioned type constraints map directly to domain/range). No nested types, subtype hierarchies, or n-ary relations are tested.\n\n3. **CF trade-off undermines guarantee framing (W6).** The paper is transparent about this (the CF-DR trade-off ablation is admirably honest), but R2 is correct that introducing up to 12-21% CF while claiming \"formal guarantees\" is a framing tension. I noted this in my original review as well. The paper's own data shows DR+CF total violations can exceed baseline violations in some configurations, which complicates the narrative.\n\n4. **Related work coverage is strong (S6).** Agree. This is one of the paper's genuine strengths. The positioning against grammar-constrained decoding, type-constrained code generation, and post-hoc methods is thorough and well-organized.\n\n5. **Comprehensive experimental coverage for an engineering contribution (S4).** Agree. 3 models x 3 datasets, four-way ablation, fine-tuning composition, beam search, latency profiling, post-hoc comparison. The experimental thoroughness substantially exceeds what most papers at this novelty level provide.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n1. **Enabling dependency is \"architecturally obvious\" (W4).** R2 dismisses this too quickly. While the logical necessity is clear in hindsight, the paper provides useful quantitative evidence: semantic-only achieves only 24% DR reduction vs. 94% for the full combination, and the gap traces specifically to OOV relation types that prevent rule lookup. The empirical demonstration that semantic constraints alone are largely ineffective is a useful finding for practitioners, even if the architectural reason is predictable. It also validates the two-layer decomposition as necessary rather than just convenient.\n\n2. **Violation landscape study not a standalone contribution (W3).** R2 is partially right that two ontologies with one primary model is thin. However, the per-relation tightness analysis (42.4% for Compare vs. 0.8% for Used-for) and the dual-factor characterization (tightness x baseline RV) provide actionable insights for when constrained decoding will and will not help. The paper is appropriately cautious, noting the dual-factor fit relies on four data points and calling it \"descriptive characterization rather than a confirmatory statistical test.\" I consider this a useful analytical contribution, though I agree it would be stronger with more ontologies.\n\n3. **Missing IterGen positioning (W5).** I did not flag this in my initial review. However, IterGen addresses a different problem setting (iterative backtracking for code generation with complex specifications). OntoCoDec explicitly avoids backtracking in favor of forward-only single-pass decoding with grammar-first fallback. A brief positioning paragraph would be appropriate but the omission is not fatal, as the mechanisms and target tasks differ substantially.\n\n### New Observations\n\n1. R2's question about **subtype hierarchies** is well-taken and something I underweighted. Real-world ontologies (biomedical, legal) have deep subtype structures. The current framework's flat enum approach would require enumerating all leaf types, which could explode the grammar size. This is a genuine limitation of the \"universal\" claim.\n\n2. R2's observation about **GoLLIE-style code-based schema encoding** as an alternative composability test is relevant. The paper only tests LoRA composition. Code-based schema encoding (where the schema is embedded in the prompt format itself) represents a fundamentally different approach to schema compliance, and testing whether OntoCoDec adds value on top of it would strengthen the composability story.\n\n3. R2 correctly identifies the **Mistral anomaly** (56.94% DR) as needing more explanation. The paper attributes it to \"poor zero-shot calibration\" but does not analyze why Mistral behaves so differently from the other two models at similar parameter scale. This weakens the cross-model generalization claim.\n\n### Updated Score: 3.5/6 (previously 4/6)\n\n### Justification for change:\nR2's review crystallized two concerns I had flagged but insufficiently penalized: (1) the novelty increment over type-constrained code generation is genuinely narrow, with the semantic layer being a lookup table with stateful tracking rather than a conceptually new mechanism; (2) the \"universal/guarantee\" framing is undermined by both the limited task coverage and the CF trade-off, which the paper acknowledges but the framing does not adequately reflect. The experimental thoroughness and honest treatment of limitations remain strong, placing this at the boundary between Findings and a reject-resubmit, leaning toward Findings."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: afd0ad5d9d6268787 (use SendMessage with to: 'afd0ad5d9d6268787' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 47754\ntool_uses: 14\nduration_ms: 75277</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0eddf8e3-65e4-4850-9e66-a5c0b106b0fa","uuid":"353b021f-67c7-4904-bc8f-a8ce90e57852","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:17:55.052Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), focusing on Methods and Experiments. You previously reviewed \"OntoCoDec: Universal Ontology-Constrained Structured Generation for Information Extraction.\" Your preliminary score was 4/6.\n\nNow read Reviewer 2's (R2) review below and respond. Specifically:\n1. Which of R2's points do you agree/disagree with?\n2. Does R2's review change your assessment? If so, how?\n3. Any points R2 raised that you missed, or vice versa?\n4. State your updated score (or confirm no change) with brief justification.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents OntoCoDec, a framework that compiles IE ontology schemas into two composable decoding-time constraint layers: a grammar layer (CFG via XGrammar) for structural/vocabulary validity, and a semantic layer (stateful entity registry + dynamic logit masking) for cross-field domain/range type constraints. The paper includes a violation landscape study, ablation experiments on SciERC, DocRED, and WikiEvents across three model families, and analysis of the enabling dependency between layers. DR violations are reduced by 42-99% with no significant F1 cost.\n\n### Strengths\n1. Well-motivated problem with clear formalization. The constraint tightness metric, violation taxonomy, and decidability taxonomy are cleanly presented.\n2. Thorough ablation design that isolates each layer's contribution. The latent violation surfacing finding on DocRED is genuinely interesting.\n3. Honest treatment of limitations. CF-DR trade-off ablation is admirably transparent.\n4. Comprehensive experimental coverage for an engineering contribution.\n5. Cross-task generalization to EE is compelling. Post-hoc filtering non-viability for EE (99.6% discard) is strong.\n6. Related work coverage is notably comprehensive, citing PICARD, Synchromesh, MGD, code generation type-constrained approaches, and post-hoc validation work.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. Core novelty is incremental relative to type-constrained code generation. The \"type system to automaton to logits mask\" pipeline exists in code generation (PLDI 2025, Synchromesh, MGD). The semantic layer is essentially a stateful logits processor with a lookup table. Useful but not deeply novel.\n2. \"Universal\" and \"task-agnostic\" claims are overclaimed. Only two IE tasks tested, both with flat type constraint structures. No nested/hierarchical types, no subtype hierarchies, no n-ary relations, no tasks beyond IE.\n3. The violation landscape study is not a standalone contribution at the level claimed. Only two ontologies (7 vs. 96 relations) with one primary model. Dual-factor analysis relies on four data points.\n4. The enabling dependency is architecturally obvious. If the semantic layer looks up rules by relation name and the name might be OOV, then of course grammar is prerequisite.\n5. Missing positioning against IterGen (Ugare et al., ICLR 2025), which does iterative semantic-aware structured generation with backtracking.\n6. CF trade-off undermines the \"guarantee\" framing. System introduces up to 12-21% CF while eliminating DR.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. How does OntoCoDec handle ontologies with subtype hierarchies?\n2. Continuation masking scaling complexity as entity types grow?\n3. Why not make beam search the primary recommendation?\n4. Why Mistral-7B shows dramatically different behavior (56.94% DR)?\n5. Composability tested only with LoRA — what about GoLLIE-style code-based schema encoding?\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Justification\nThe paper addresses a real problem with thorough experiments and clear practical value. However, core technical novelty is limited — the two-layer architecture is a natural extension of existing constrained decoding. The enabling dependency is architecturally obvious. \"Universal\" claim not supported by two tasks. CF trade-off partially undermines guarantee framing. Solid engineering contribution at the boundary between Findings and main conference.\n\n---\n\nYour response format:\n```\n## R1 Response to R2\n\n### Points of Agreement\n[numbered list]\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n[numbered list]\n\n### New Observations\n[any points R2 raised that you find particularly relevant or missed]\n\n### Updated Score: X/6 (previously 4/6)\n### Justification for change (or no change): [1-2 sentences]\n```\n","agentId":"afd0ad5d9d6268787","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me formulate my response as R1.\n\n## R1 Response to R2\n\n### Points of Agreement\n\n1. **Incremental novelty relative to type-constrained code generation (W1).** I agree this is the paper's central vulnerability. The \"type system to automaton to logit mask\" pipeline has clear antecedents. The authors do articulate why IE differs (dynamic entity registry, context-dependent constraint targets, non-rectangular type signatures), and the continuation masking for tokenizer-agnostic enforcement is non-trivial engineering. But the conceptual leap is modest. I flagged this in my initial review as well.\n\n2. **\"Universal\" overclaim (W2).** Two IE tasks (RE and EE) with flat type hierarchies do not justify \"universal\" or \"task-agnostic.\" I agree. The WikiEvents extension is valuable but structurally similar (role-conditioned type constraints map directly to domain/range). No nested types, subtype hierarchies, or n-ary relations are tested.\n\n3. **CF trade-off undermines guarantee framing (W6).** The paper is transparent about this (the CF-DR trade-off ablation is admirably honest), but R2 is correct that introducing up to 12-21% CF while claiming \"formal guarantees\" is a framing tension. I noted this in my original review as well. The paper's own data shows DR+CF total violations can exceed baseline violations in some configurations, which complicates the narrative.\n\n4. **Related work coverage is strong (S6).** Agree. This is one of the paper's genuine strengths. The positioning against grammar-constrained decoding, type-constrained code generation, and post-hoc methods is thorough and well-organized.\n\n5. **Comprehensive experimental coverage for an engineering contribution (S4).** Agree. 3 models x 3 datasets, four-way ablation, fine-tuning composition, beam search, latency profiling, post-hoc comparison. The experimental thoroughness substantially exceeds what most papers at this novelty level provide.\n\n### Points of Disagreement\n\n1. **Enabling dependency is \"architecturally obvious\" (W4).** R2 dismisses this too quickly. While the logical necessity is clear in hindsight, the paper provides useful quantitative evidence: semantic-only achieves only 24% DR reduction vs. 94% for the full combination, and the gap traces specifically to OOV relation types that prevent rule lookup. The empirical demonstration that semantic constraints alone are largely ineffective is a useful finding for practitioners, even if the architectural reason is predictable. It also validates the two-layer decomposition as necessary rather than just convenient.\n\n2. **Violation landscape study not a standalone contribution (W3).** R2 is partially right that two ontologies with one primary model is thin. However, the per-relation tightness analysis (42.4% for Compare vs. 0.8% for Used-for) and the dual-factor characterization (tightness x baseline RV) provide actionable insights for when constrained decoding will and will not help. The paper is appropriately cautious, noting the dual-factor fit relies on four data points and calling it \"descriptive characterization rather than a confirmatory statistical test.\" I consider this a useful analytical contribution, though I agree it would be stronger with more ontologies.\n\n3. **Missing IterGen positioning (W5).** I did not flag this in my initial review. However, IterGen addresses a different problem setting (iterative backtracking for code generation with complex specifications). OntoCoDec explicitly avoids backtracking in favor of forward-only single-pass decoding with grammar-first fallback. A brief positioning paragraph would be appropriate but the omission is not fatal, as the mechanisms and target tasks differ substantially.\n\n### New Observations\n\n1. R2's question about **subtype hierarchies** is well-taken and something I underweighted. Real-world ontologies (biomedical, legal) have deep subtype structures. The current framework's flat enum approach would require enumerating all leaf types, which could explode the grammar size. This is a genuine limitation of the \"universal\" claim.\n\n2. R2's observation about **GoLLIE-style code-based schema encoding** as an alternative composability test is relevant. The paper only tests LoRA composition. Code-based schema encoding (where the schema is embedded in the prompt format itself) represents a fundamentally different approach to schema compliance, and testing whether OntoCoDec adds value on top of it would strengthen the composability story.\n\n3. R2 correctly identifies the **Mistral anomaly** (56.94% DR) as needing more explanation. The paper attributes it to \"poor zero-shot calibration\" but does not analyze why Mistral behaves so differently from the other two models at similar parameter scale. This weakens the cross-model generalization claim.\n\n### Updated Score: 3.5/6 (previously 4/6)\n\n### Justification for change:\nR2's review crystallized two concerns I had flagged but insufficiently penalized: (1) the novelty increment over type-constrained code generation is genuinely narrow, with the semantic layer being a lookup table with stateful tracking rather than a conceptually new mechanism; (2) the \"universal/guarantee\" framing is undermined by both the limited task coverage and the CF trade-off, which the paper acknowledges but the framing does not adequately reflect. The experimental thoroughness and honest treatment of limitations remain strong, placing this at the boundary between Findings and a reject-resubmit, leaning toward Findings."}],"totalDurationMs":75277,"totalTokens":47754,"totalToolUseCount":14,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12844,"cache_read_input_tokens":33637,"output_tokens":1272,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12844},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1272,"cache_read_input_tokens":33637,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12844,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12844,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}}}
