{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017hM7NmdwccMeN4wZWzGyKh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Uw2hjtsJTtwXK4xEFYzAQk","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: OntoCoDec\n\n**Paper**: \"OntoCoDec: Universal Ontology-Constrained Structured Generation for Information Extraction\"\n**Review mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n**Review standard**: Top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nOntoCoDec compiles IE ontology schemas into two composable decoding-time constraint layers: (1) a grammar layer (CFG via XGrammar) enforcing well-formed JSON and vocabulary membership, and (2) a semantic layer (stateful entity registry with dynamic logit masking) enforcing domain/range constraints (RE) and role-argument type constraints (EE). The two layers compose via bitwise AND of token masks, with graceful degradation to grammar-only when the intersection is empty. The paper includes a violation landscape study across SciERC, DocRED, and WikiEvents, demonstrating that LLMs violate ontology constraints at 6.5-26% (domain/range) and up to 89% (role vocabulary). OntoCoDec reduces DR violations by 44-100% across 2 tasks, 4 models (3x8B + 1x70B), and 3 datasets, while eliminating vocabulary-level violations entirely and maintaining F1 scores.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reviewer Scores\n\n| Reviewer | Focus | Initial | Post-Discussion | Confidence |\n|----------|-------|---------|-----------------|------------|\n| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 4/6 | 3.5/6 | 4/5 |\n| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 4/6 | 4/6 | 4/5 |\n| **Average** | | | **3.75/6** | |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consensus Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-defined problem with clear formalization.** The violation taxonomy (DR, RV, ET, CF, EV, RoV, AC), constraint tightness metric, and decidability classification (purely local / prefix-decidable with state / truly non-local) provide a clean conceptual framework. The problem is real and well-motivated: downstream KG construction, reasoning engines, and compliance pipelines need ontology-valid outputs. Both reviewers agree the problem matters.\n\n**S2. Thorough ablation design.** The four-way ablation (unconstrained, grammar-only, semantic-only, full S1) cleanly isolates each layer's contribution. The \"latent violation surfacing\" finding on DocRED (grammar-only DR *increases* from 18.46% to 26.17% as OOV predictions become checkable) is a genuinely insightful observation that both reviewers flagged as a highlight. The enabling dependency (semantic-only: 24% DR reduction vs. full system: 94%) is empirically validated even if architecturally predictable.\n\n**S3. Experimental breadth.** 3 model families, 3 datasets, 2 IE tasks, 4 constraint configurations, fine-tuning composability, beam search, sampling, post-hoc filtering baseline, prompt sensitivity, document length analysis, latency profiling, and 10,000-iteration bootstrap CIs. The coverage substantially exceeds expectations for the novelty level.\n\n**S4. Honest treatment of limitations.** The CF trade-off is not hidden. The CF-DR trade-off ablation (showing eliminating CF re-routes +13.85pp to DR) is admirably transparent. The dual-factor analysis (tightness x RV baseline) explicitly notes n=4 and refrains from overclaiming. The paper is more forthcoming about weaknesses than most submissions.\n\n**S5. Cross-task generalization to EE is compelling.** Applying the same framework to WikiEvents with zero architectural modification, eliminating EV and RoV violations entirely across all 3 models, and demonstrating that post-hoc filtering is catastrophically non-viable for EE (99.6-100% discard) is the paper's strongest empirical result.\n\n**S6. Comprehensive related work.** The paper positions itself against grammar-constrained decoding (XGrammar, Outlines, LMQL), type-constrained code generation (PICARD, Synchromesh, MGD), training-based type-aware IE (UIE, GoLLIE, KnowCoder), and post-hoc validation (ODKE+, SPIRES). The coverage is thorough.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Consensus Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Aggregate violation metric reveals modest net improvement.** [Both reviewers, R1 primary] DR+CF total violations under S1 on SciERC: 10.83% vs. 15.45% unconstrained, a ~30% total reduction. The headline \"94% DR reduction\" foregrounds one violation type while the system introduces another (CF: 0.08% to 9.90%). On WikiEvents EE, CF reaches 14.9-21.1%. The paper never reports aggregate violation metrics. **Recommendation**: Report DR+CF (or total violation rate across all types) as a primary metric alongside individual rates.\n\n**W2. \"Formal guarantee\" framing is overclaimed.** [Both reviewers] The abstract and introduction use \"formal guarantees\" and \"type-safety guarantees,\" but: (a) DocRED residual DR is 10.21% (Qwen), far from a guarantee; (b) CF violations are introduced at up to 21%; (c) the fallback mechanism explicitly degrades to grammar-only, abandoning semantic enforcement. The guarantee is real for structural validity (well-formed JSON, valid vocabulary) but only probabilistic for semantic type constraints. **Recommendation**: Distinguish clearly between structural guarantees (real) and semantic violation reduction (statistical improvement, not guarantee).\n\n**W3. Core novelty is incremental.** [R2 primary, R1 concurs] The \"type system to automaton to logit mask\" pipeline exists in code generation (Synchromesh, PICARD, PLDI 2025 type-constrained codegen). The semantic layer is a stateful logits processor with a lookup table. The IE-specific adaptation (dynamic entity registry, context-dependent constraints) is valid engineering but not a conceptual breakthrough. The enabling dependency, while empirically useful, follows directly from the architecture (if semantic layer looks up rules by relation name and names may be OOV, grammar is obviously a prerequisite). **Recommendation**: Frame contribution more honestly as \"adapting and validating an established paradigm for IE\" rather than implying greater novelty.\n\n**W4. \"Universal\" / \"task-agnostic\" claim is overclaimed.** [Both reviewers] Only two IE tasks (RE, EE) with flat type hierarchies are tested. No nested/hierarchical entity types, no subtype hierarchies, no n-ary relations, no tasks beyond IE, no web-scale ontologies. Two tasks do not establish universality. **Recommendation**: Soften title and claims, or add at least one additional task type (e.g., schema-guided dialogue, structured summarization).\n\n**W5. Zero-shot F1 (2-11%) limits practical significance assessment.** [R1 primary, R2 concurs] At such low extraction quality, most generated triples are incorrect. The violation rate among true-positive triples vs. false-positive triples is never reported. If violations concentrate in FP triples (which will be discarded anyway), practical impact is diminished. Fine-tuned experiments partially address this but are limited in scope (LoRA only, not tested with GoLLIE-style code-based schemas). **Recommendation**: Report violation rates stratified by TP/FP status.\n\n**W6. Missing baselines.** [R1 primary, R2 concurs] (a) No comparison with EnriCo, DORE, or GenIE runtime violation rates despite discussing them in Related Work. (b) Post-hoc *correction* (reject + re-generate until compliant) is the natural strong baseline but is not tested. The paper only compares against post-hoc *filtering* (discard violating triples). (c) No evaluation on biomedical or other domain-specific ontologies despite motivating with safety-critical domains. (d) IterGen (Ugare et al., ICLR 2025), which does iterative semantic-aware structured generation with backtracking, is cited but never discussed in the related work text.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Points of Divergence\n\n**D1. Severity of the enabling dependency as a contribution.** R2 considers it \"architecturally obvious\" and overstated as a key finding. R1 acknowledges the architectural predictability but argues the quantitative evidence (24% vs. 94%) is useful for practitioners. **Resolution**: Both agree it is a valid empirical finding but not a conceptual insight. It should be presented as validating the architecture rather than as a discovery.\n\n**D2. Weight of conceptual contributions.** R2 values the decidability taxonomy, tightness formalization, and non-rectangularity analysis as non-trivial conceptual contributions that elevate the paper above pure engineering. R1 weighs these less and focuses on the execution-level modesty (bitwise AND of two logit masks with fallback). **Resolution**: Both agree these formalizations are useful; they disagree on whether they lift the paper from Findings to Main conference level.\n\n**D3. 70B results.** R1 flags the commented-out 70B paragraph as an inconsistency. R2 considers this a non-issue since the placeholder is not compiled into the submitted version. **Resolution**: The cross-scale claim in Contribution 4 is supported by the 70B SciERC results that are in the paper (Table 6); the DocRED 70B data appears to still be running. This is a minor concern.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors (Consolidated)\n\n1. **Aggregate violation rate**: What is the total violation rate (DR+RV+ET+CF) under each configuration? The per-type reporting obscures the net improvement.\n\n2. **TP vs. FP violation breakdown**: What fraction of violations occur in true-positive triples vs. false-positive triples? This is critical for assessing practical impact.\n\n3. **Subtype hierarchies**: How does OntoCoDec handle ontologies with is-a hierarchies (e.g., Person subclass-of Agent)? This is common in real-world ontologies and not addressed.\n\n4. **Post-hoc correction baseline**: Has reject-and-regenerate been considered? This is the natural strong baseline for constrained generation.\n\n5. **Beam search as primary recommendation**: LoRA + beam + S1 appears to achieve near-zero violations across all types. Why is greedy S1 the primary recommendation rather than beam?\n\n6. **Mistral anomaly**: Why does Mistral-7B show 56.94% DR on SciERC vs. 13-15% for other models at similar scale? This substantially affects the reported DR reduction ranges.\n\n7. **Continuation masking scalability**: What is the compile-time and runtime complexity as entity types grow beyond 27? The paper notes this is unverified.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper should report aggregate violation metrics (sum of all types) as a primary metric\n- WikiEvents grammar-only F1 shown as \"---\" but should be measurable\n- SciERC ablation has S2 row but DocRED does not; asymmetry unexplained\n- 0.03% residual RV (\"chimeric values\") understates potential issues for ontologies with more prefix-overlapping names\n- DocRED uses dev set; not discussed as a generalizability limitation\n- Sampling shows DocRED CF jumping to 12.63% on 114 docs; deserves more discussion\n- The Conclusion is very compressed (3 sentences per R2 observation); brief discussion of implications would strengthen\n- Abstract is dense with numbers (234 words); narrative arc could be clearer\n\n---\n\n## 8. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing against the 35 planned experiments in review_context.yaml:\n\n| Category | Planned | Covered | Notes |\n|----------|---------|---------|-------|\n| Violation landscape (P001-P004) | 4 | 4 | Full coverage in Section 3 |\n| SciERC ablation (P005a-P008) | 4 | 4 | Full coverage in Section 5.2, Appendix B |\n| DocRED ablation (P009-P012) | 4 | 4 | Full coverage in Section 5.3 |\n| Cross-model Llama (P013-P016) | 4 | 4 | Full coverage in Section 5.4 |\n| S2 pilot (P017) | 1 | 0 | Correctly superseded by P008 |\n| Post-hoc filtering (P018) | 1 | 1 | Section 5.7 |\n| Bootstrap CI (P019) | 1 | 1 | Appendix A |\n| WikiEvents EE (P021-P028) | 8 | 8 | Sections 5.6-5.7, Tables 7-9 |\n| 70B cross-scale (P029-P030) | 2 | 1.5 | SciERC complete (Table 6); DocRED was RUNNING |\n| Error analysis (P031) | 1 | 1 | Sections 6.4-6.5 |\n| EE FT composability (P032) | 1 | 1 | Section 5.8 |\n| EE grammar-only cross-model (P033) | 1 | 1 | Table 8 |\n| EE bootstrap CI (P034) | 1 | 1 | Appendix A Table 11 |\n| Latency (P035) | 1 | 1 | Section 5.9, Appendix D |\n\n**Coverage**: 33.5/35 experiments reflected in the paper. Excellent coverage. The only gap is the 70B DocRED experiment which was still running.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.75/6 (Weak Accept, leaning toward Findings)**\n\nThis paper addresses a well-defined, practical problem and delivers a working system with comprehensive empirical validation. The experimental thoroughness is a clear strength, and the honest treatment of limitations is commendable. The cross-task generalization from RE to EE with zero architectural modification is the most compelling result.\n\nHowever, the core technical novelty is incremental relative to existing type-constrained decoding in code generation. The semantic layer is a stateful logit processor that adapts an established paradigm to IE. The enabling dependency, while empirically validated, is architecturally predictable. The \"universal\" and \"formal guarantee\" claims overshoot what the experiments demonstrate: two tasks with flat type hierarchies, and a system that introduces up to 21% CF violations while reducing DR.\n\nThe most significant gap is the absence of aggregate violation reporting: when DR+CF is computed, the net improvement is substantially more modest than the headline DR reduction figures suggest (~30% total reduction on SciERC rather than 94%). This reframing materially affects the paper's value proposition.\n\n**Verdict**: A solid engineering contribution with useful empirical insights and formalizations, suitable for Findings-level publication. To reach Main conference, the paper would need to: (1) report and discuss aggregate violation metrics honestly, (2) tone down the guarantee and universality framing, (3) include a post-hoc correction baseline, and (4) either strengthen the novelty claim with additional task types or more explicitly frame the contribution as an engineering advance.\n\n**Mapping to EMNLP 2026 ARR scale**: Overall Assessment ~3.0-3.5 (Findings-level). The paper has clear merit and would likely be accepted at Findings if the framing issues (guarantee overclaim, missing aggregate metrics) are addressed in revision.","project":"schema_semantic_constrained_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260518T162745","overall_score":"borderline accept (3.75/6), Findings-level","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":667,"cache_read_input_tokens":78666,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":667},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0eddf8e3-65e4-4850-9e66-a5c0b106b0fa","uuid":"3c5d1bf6-7c21-4676-9875-30eae4712d37"}
